{"name":"Dress Blues","hex":"#2a3244","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dress","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To put clothes (or, formerly, armour) on (oneself or someone, a doll, a mannequin, etc.); to clothe. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","To put clothes (or, formerly, armour) on (oneself or someone, a doll, a mannequin, etc.); to clothe. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An item of clothing (usually worn by a woman or young girl) which both covers the upper part of the body and includes a skirt below the waist.","An item of clothing (usually worn by a woman or young girl) which both covers the upper part of the body and includes a skirt below the waist."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dress"},{"term":"blues","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of blue"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A feeling of sadness or depression.","One's particular life experience, particularly including the hardships one has faced."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blues"}],"entries":[{"title":"Uniforms of the British Army","blocks":[{"text":"The uniforms of the British Army currently exist in twelve categories ranging from ceremonial uniforms to combat dress. Uniforms in the British Army are specific to the regiment to which a soldier belongs. Full dress presents the most differentiation between units, and there are fewer regimental distinctions between ceremonial dress, service dress, barrack dress and combat dress, though a level of regimental distinction runs throughout."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Soldier%27s_Kit_Locker_MOD_45158195.jpg/330px-Soldier%27s_Kit_Locker_MOD_45158195.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uniforms_of_the_British_Army"},{"title":"Uniforms of the Royal Marines","blocks":[{"text":"The Royal Marines uniform is the standardised military dress worn by members of the Royal Marines."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/The_Royal_Marines_from_1664_to_1896.jpg/330px-The_Royal_Marines_from_1664_to_1896.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uniforms_of_the_Royal_Marines"},{"title":"Dress","blocks":[{"text":"A dress is a one-piece outer garment that is worn on the torso, hanging down over the legs. Dresses often consist of a bodice attached to a skirt."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Afternoon_ensemble_MET_63.212a-b_CP4.jpg/330px-Afternoon_ensemble_MET_63.212a-b_CP4.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dress"},{"title":"Blues","blocks":[{"text":"Blues is a music genre and musical form that originated among African Americans in the Deep South of the United States around the 1860s. Blues has incorporated spirituals, work songs, field hollers, shouts, chants, and rhymed simple narrative ballads from the African-American culture. The blues form is ubiquitous in jazz, rhythm and blues, and rock and roll, and is characterized by the call-and-response pattern, the blues scale, and specific chord progressions, of which the twelve-bar blues is the most common. Blue notes, usually thirds, fifths or sevenths flattened in pitch, are also an essential part of the sound. Blues shuffles or walking bass reinforce the trance-like rhythm and form a repetitive effect known as the groove."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blues"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/DWR_Colours%2C_Erquinghem_Lys%2C_France_%28RLH%29_2005-11-12-2.jpg/500px-DWR_Colours%2C_Erquinghem_Lys%2C_France_%28RLH%29_2005-11-12-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/DWR_Colours%2C_Erquinghem_Lys%2C_France_%28RLH%29_2005-11-12-2.jpg/500px-DWR_Colours%2C_Erquinghem_Lys%2C_France_%28RLH%29_2005-11-12-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Line infantry full dress (Duke of Wellington's Regiment): Regimental Drummers scarlet full dress tunic of pre–World War I pattern, Home Service helmet of 1878.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADWR_Colours%2C_Erquinghem_Lys%2C_France_(RLH)_2005-11-12-2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/The_King%27s_Troop_Royal_Horse_Artillery_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1464992.jpg/500px-The_King%27s_Troop_Royal_Horse_Artillery_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1464992.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/The_King%27s_Troop_Royal_Horse_Artillery_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1464992.jpg/500px-The_King%27s_Troop_Royal_Horse_Artillery_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1464992.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Troopers of the King's Troop, Royal Horse Artillery in their blue light cavalry-style full dress uniform","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_King's_Troop_Royal_Horse_Artillery_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1464992.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Commemoration_of_the_Battle_of_Montcel_%C3%A0_Fr%C3%A9toy.jpg/500px-Commemoration_of_the_Battle_of_Montcel_%C3%A0_Fr%C3%A9toy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Commemoration_of_the_Battle_of_Montcel_%C3%A0_Fr%C3%A9toy.jpg/500px-Commemoration_of_the_Battle_of_Montcel_%C3%A0_Fr%C3%A9toy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Lancers forming a guard of honour in full dress","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACommemoration_of_the_Battle_of_Montcel_%C3%A0_Fr%C3%